## std/sched — the `Sched` algebraic effect for foreign-thread callback contexts.
##
## A function whose signature carries `can Sched` runs in a context that is
## not the lexical caller's context — typically a scheduler-managed pthread
## (the I/O poller), an interrupt service routine, or a panic handler.
##
## The presence of `can Sched` on a function signature is a typecheck-only
## marker. It declares no operations, has no runtime handler, and incurs zero
## emission cost. What it DOES do is trigger a structural Sync-bound check on
## every captured / global access inside the function body — when the body is
## walked under `can Sched`, reading a non-`Sync` value (bare `Vec<T>`,
## `Map<K,V>`, `Set<T>`, `StringBuilder`, etc.) emits QZ1408. Foreign-thread
## execution does not synchronize with main-thread mutators on weakly-ordered
## hardware (ARM64), so the check is structural rather than dynamic.
##
## To pass the check: wrap shared state in `Mutex<T>` / `Atomic*<T>` and access
## via `.lock()` / `.load()` (the returned local is fresh stack-state and
## exempt), or move the binding to per-thread storage with `@thread_local`.
##
## Sites that take `can Sched` callbacks:
##
##   * `sched_idle_hook_set(f: Fn(): Void can Sched)` — registers `f` to run
##     once per event-loop tick on the I/O poller pthread.
##   * `extern "x86-interrupt" def *_isr(...): Void can Sched` — IRQ handlers.
##   * `@panic_handler def my_panic(msg: String): Void can Sched` — runs on
##     whatever thread panicked.
##
## Adding a new foreign-thread callback API: declare the callback parameter
## as `Fn(...): T can Sched`. The Sync discipline cascades automatically.
##
## See `docs/design/SCHED_EFFECT_THREAD_SAFETY.md` for design rationale,
## prior-art survey, and PSQ-6 closure context.

## `effect Sched end` — no ops. Marker semantics live entirely at the
## typecheck layer (the `can Sched` annotation triggers the structural
## Sync check on body captures + globals via `tc_type_is_sync`).
effect Sched
end
